+# ttlcache: summary
+
+## Result
+
+`python -m unittest` runs 42 tests and all pass: the 9 original tests in `tests/test_cache.py` (unchanged) and 33 new
+tests in `tests/test_contract.py`. The original 9 errored before this change because `TTLCache` did not accept `clock=`.
+
+## What was wrong
+
+The original `get` in `ttlcache/cache.py` had no record of loads in flight, so two threads that missed at the same
+time both called the loader. It also read `_values` and `_stamps` without a lock and wrote them in two separate
+steps, so a reader could pair a new value with an old timestamp and serve an expired value. Its expiry test was
+`<=`, so an entry exactly `ttl` old was still served, which the contract says is expired. It used `time.monotonic`
+directly instead of the injected clock, and it treated `None` as "not cached".
+
+These are the likely causes of the two reported symptoms. I did not reproduce the field reports themselves.
+
+## What changed
+
+- `ttlcache/cache.py`: rewritten. One lock with a condition variable guards all state. The loader runs without the
+  lock. A `_loads` map tracks loads in flight, so one loader call serves all concurrent misses (guarantee 1). Waiters
+  are woken on completion, failure, or timeout. The new options are implemented: `clock`, `max_size` (LRU over stored
+  entries, never evicting a key with a load in flight), `stale_grace` (stale value on failure, read once when the
+  failure is recorded), and `timeout` on `get`. Deadlock detection walks a thread-to-waited-load graph under the lock.
+  Recursion (guarantee 5) raises `RuntimeError`. Argument validation raises `ValueError`.
+- `ttlcache/errors.py` (new): `DeadlockError(RuntimeError)`.
+- `ttlcache/stats.py`: adds `evictions`, `stale_served`, `timeouts`, `deadlocks`; `snapshot(extended=False)`.
+- `ttlcache/__init__.py`: exports `DeadlockError`.
+- `tests/test_contract.py` (new): tests for validation, clock boundaries, single load under concurrency, failure
+  identity, invalidate during load, `len`, independence, recursion, timeouts, stale serving, LRU, deadlock detection,
+  and exact stats under concurrency.
+
+## What I ran
+
+- `python -m unittest` (Python 3.14.3): 42 tests, OK. Before the change: 9 errors.
+- Repeated `python -m unittest` 30 times in a loop: 30 of 30 exit code 0.
+- Stress: the new and original test modules, 15 runs, with `sys.setswitchinterval(1e-6)`: 15 of 15 OK.
+
+## Not verified / open points
+
+- I did not run any CI configuration. The repo has none, so I only ran the command you gave.
+- Only Python 3.14 was tested. The concurrency tests use real threads and real timeouts (0.05 s, plus a 10 s
+  join guard). They are not timing-sensitive in the failure direction, but a very slow machine could in principle
+  hit the 10 s guard.
+- `test_contract.py` has a white-box helper that reads `cache._cond` and `cache._waiting` to know when a waiter has
+  registered. Those tests will need updating if the internals change.
+- Judgement calls where the contract is not explicit:
+  - `bool` is rejected for `ttl`, `max_size` and `stale_grace`, and non-numbers raise `ValueError`.
+  - A `BaseException` (e.g. `KeyboardInterrupt`) from the loader is recorded as a failure like any other, and it can
+    be stale-served.
+  - `clock()` is called while the lock is held, so a clock that calls back into the cache would deadlock.
+  - Timeouts are clamped to `threading.TIMEOUT_MAX`, so an `inf` timeout waits effectively forever.
+- Deadlock detection only sees cycles made of `get` waits. A cycle that goes through some other lock is not detected.
+- `README.md` was not changed (outside the allowed paths). It does not mention the extended stats keys or the
+  `DeadlockError` class. Nothing else needs changing.
+- Nothing has been committed. The changes are in the working tree.
